        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 18, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the costs below.</strong> Parts and labour figures are
          typical US ranges as of September 2026, gathered from parts suppliers and service pricing
          rather than from a single source, and they vary considerably by region, brand and whether
          a part is generic or proprietary. Use them to judge whether a quote is reasonable, not as
          a quotation. Always unplug the machine before opening any panel.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-breaks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. What Actually Breaks, in Order</a>
          <a href="#check-first" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The Free Checks, Before You Call Anyone</a>
          <a href="#what-it-costs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. What Repairs Actually Cost</a>
          <a href="#diy-vs-pro" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Where the DIY Line Actually Sits</a>
          <a href="#finding-a-technician" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Finding a Technician Who Is Worth Calling</a>
          <a href="#by-brand" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Notes by Brand</a>
          <a href="#repair-or-replace" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Repair or Replace: The Arithmetic</a>
          <a href="#preventing" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Preventing the Next One</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A treadmill is a motor, a board, two rollers and a belt sliding on a board. That is
          genuinely most of it, and it means the list of things that can go wrong is short, the
          cheap failures vastly outnumber the expensive ones, and a surprising share of dead
          treadmills are not broken in any meaningful sense.
        </p>

        <div id="what-breaks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What Actually Breaks, in Order</h2>
<p>
            Ordered by how often it happens, which is almost the inverse of how much it costs — a
            useful thing to know before you assume the worst.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Nothing at all.</strong> The single most common cause of a treadmill that will
            not start is a safety key whose magnet has weakened, or one not seated properly. It is
            also the cheapest thing on this list. See the next section before doing anything else.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lubrication, or the lack of it.</strong> A belt slipping, hesitating under load,
            or a machine that has become noisy and feels like it is struggling is very often a dry
            deck rather than a failing anything. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication
            guide</a> is the first thing to try on any machine that feels tired.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt tension and tracking.</strong> Belts stretch and wander. Both are adjusted
            with a hex key in minutes —
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">centring</a>
            and <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tensioning</a>
            cover it — and both cause real damage if ignored.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Wiring at the fold pivot.</strong> On a folding machine, the loom crosses a
            hinge that has flexed thousands of times. Conductors break inside intact-looking
            insulation, and the symptom is intermittent power or a console that dies when the deck
            moves. Cheap to fix, frequently misdiagnosed as a dead board.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Running belt and deck wear.</strong> Genuine consumables. A worn belt is a
            negotiating point rather than a catastrophe, and our
            <a href="/how-often-should-a-treadmill-belt-be-replaced/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt
            replacement guide</a> covers the interval.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor controller board.</strong> The first genuinely expensive failure, and
            usually the downstream consequence of years of friction rather than a fault of its own.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Drive motor.</strong> Expensive, and nearly always killed by the same friction.
            Our <a href="/how-long-do-treadmill-motors-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">motor
            lifespan guide</a> covers the mechanism in detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Console or upper board.</strong> Expensive, model-specific, and the hardest part
            to source on anything discontinued.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="check-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Free Checks, Before You Call Anyone</h2>
<p>
            Work through these in order. They cost nothing, take about ten minutes, and resolve a
            significant share of apparent failures. Unplug the machine before removing any panel.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>One. The safety key.</strong> Remove it, reseat it firmly, try again. If you have
            a spare, try that. Magnets weaken and keys crack, and this single component accounts for
            an enormous number of dead consoles. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-wont-turn-on/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">power fault guide</a>
            covers how to confirm it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Two. The outlet and the breaker.</strong> Test the outlet with something else.
            Check the machine's own reset breaker, usually a small button near the power inlet, and
            the household breaker. Remove any extension lead from the equation entirely — treadmills
            draw more than most people assume and are unhappy on thin extensions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Three. Fold and unfold it while watching the console.</strong> If the display
            flickers or dies as the deck moves, you have found a broken conductor at the pivot, not
            a dead board.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Four. Slide a hand between belt and deck.</strong> Slightly slick is right. Dry,
            hot or gritty means friction is high, and friction explains slipping, hesitation, noise,
            heat and a motor that is working far harder than it should. Lubricate before concluding
            anything else.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Five. Stand on it at low speed.</strong> A belt that moves freely with no load
            and hesitates with one is a tension or friction problem, not a motor problem — our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slows-down-when-i-step-on-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide
            to that specific symptom</a> covers it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Six. Listen and place the noise.</strong> A rumble rising with speed points at
            rollers or bearings; a rhythmic squeak often tracks the belt seam; a grinding from the
            front is the motor area. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">noise guide</a>
            separates them, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">full
            troubleshooting guide</a> is the systematic version of this whole section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Repairs Actually Cost</h2>
<p>
            Two components to any quote: the part, and the labour. Independent technicians commonly
            charge a call-out or diagnostic fee in the region of <strong>$75 to $150</strong>, often
            credited against the work if you proceed, with labour at a similar hourly rate.
            That fixed cost is why small repairs are disproportionately worth doing yourself.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Typical parts pricing, before labour:</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Safety key</strong> — a few dollars to about $20. <strong>Drive belt</strong>
            (the small belt from motor to roller, not the running belt) — roughly $20 to $50.
            <strong>Running belt</strong> — around $100 to $250 depending on size and quality.
            <strong>Deck board</strong> — roughly $150 to $350, and often reversible, so flipping it
            is free. <strong>Rollers</strong> — about $50 to $150 each.
            <strong>Incline motor</strong> — roughly $100 to $250.
            <strong>Motor controller board</strong> — around $100 to $300.
            <strong>Drive motor</strong> — roughly $200 to $500 for a consumer DC motor.
            <strong>Console or upper board</strong> — $150 to $500 and up, with the ceiling set by
            how proprietary the display is.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Common all-in repairs</strong> therefore land roughly like this. A belt and deck
            service with labour: a few hundred dollars. A controller board replacement: similar. A
            motor replacement: often $400 to $800 all in. A touchscreen console on a platform
            machine: potentially more than the machine is worth.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Put that against the cost of doing nothing.</strong> Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance cost
            guide</a> puts routine upkeep in the tens of dollars a year. Nearly every expensive
            repair on this page is the downstream cost of skipping that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy-vs-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Where the DIY Line Actually Sits</h2>
<p>
            Clearer than most people assume. Unplug the machine before opening anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do it yourself, confidently:</strong> lubrication, belt tensioning, belt
            centring, replacing a safety key, vacuuming the
            <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">motor
            compartment</a>, cleaning
            <a href="/how-to-clean-under-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under the
            belt</a>, tightening fasteners, and replacing a running belt or flipping a deck if you
            are reasonably practical. None of these require electrical work and all are well within
            reach of an ordinary toolkit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Reasonable to attempt</strong> if you are comfortable with hand tools and
            photograph everything before disconnecting it: roller replacement, drive belt
            replacement, motor brush replacement on machines that have serviceable brushes, and
            swapping a controller board on a like-for-like part.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Call someone</strong> for anything involving diagnosis rather than replacement,
            any mains-side wiring, a motor replacement on a heavy machine, a commercial treadmill
            with a non-domestic supply, or any situation where you are guessing which of two
            expensive parts has failed. Guessing wrong on a board costs more than the diagnostic fee.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The economics of the call-out</strong> mean it is worth batching. If a technician
            is coming for a controller board, have them check the belt, deck, rollers and tension
            while they are there. The marginal cost of the extra work is small against the fixed
            cost of the visit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="finding-a-technician" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Finding a Technician Who Is Worth Calling</h2>
<p>
            Searching for local treadmill repair is how most people start, and the results are a
            mix of excellent independents, general appliance firms who will take the job reluctantly,
            and lead-generation sites that sell your details onward.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Start with the manufacturer.</strong> Most major brands maintain a network of
            authorised servicers and will tell you who covers your area. That is the best route for
            anything in warranty, and often the best route out of warranty too, because those
            technicians have the parts channel.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Then try commercial fitness servicers.</strong> Every area with gyms has firms
            that maintain them. They are experienced, they carry stock, and many will take domestic
            work between commercial calls. For a
            <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial machine at
            home</a> — a <a href="/precor-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Precor</a>,
            a <a href="/life-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness</a> — this is
            the only route that really works.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What to ask before booking:</strong> the diagnostic fee and whether it is
            credited against the repair; whether they have worked on your brand; whether they carry
            common parts on the van or order after diagnosis, since that decides whether this is one
            visit or two; and whether the quote after diagnosis is binding.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Have your model and serial number ready.</strong> It is on a plate on the frame,
            usually near the front of the deck or on a rear crossbar. Photograph it. Nearly every
            useful conversation about parts starts there, and a technician who is given it on the
            phone can often tell you the likely fault and cost before arriving.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Be sceptical of a diagnosis given over the phone without it</strong>, and
            especially of anyone quoting a board replacement before seeing the machine. Boards are
            the most over-diagnosed component in this category, precisely because the cheap causes
            above present identically.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Notes by Brand</h2>
<p>
            Parts availability varies more than build quality does, and it is the thing that decides
            whether a given repair is even possible.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack and ProForm</strong> share a corporate parent and a parts operation,
            so components and part numbers frequently cross between them. That makes sourcing
            relatively straightforward. Console and platform-tied parts are the constraint. See our
            <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack</a> and
            <a href="/proform-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm</a> guides.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Horizon, Matrix, Vision — and now Bowflex</strong> — all sit under Johnson Health
            Tech, which means a single large parts operation behind several badges and useful
            cross-referencing between them. Our
            <a href="/bowflex-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex guide</a>
            explains how that came about.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole</strong> builds plain machines with generic-friendly components and long
            frame and motor warranties, which makes them among the easier consumer treadmills to keep
            running. See our <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole
            guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Discontinued badges</strong> are the interesting case, and usually better than
            they look. Our
            <a href="/livestrong-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LiveStrong guide</a>
            and <a href="/weslo-cadence-g-5-9-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Weslo
            review</a> both make the same point: find out who actually built the machine, then search
            under that manufacturer's part numbers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Anonymous import brands</strong> are the genuine problem. No parts channel, no
            service network, and a controller board with no equivalent. This is the strongest
            practical argument for buying a machine from a manufacturer with a support operation,
            and it is why our
            <a href="/costco-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">retail buying guide</a>
            treats curated selection as a real benefit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-or-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Repair or Replace: The Arithmetic</h2>
<p>
            A framework, because sentiment about a machine you have owned for years distorts this
            reliably.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Almost always repair</strong> a safety key, wiring, lubrication-related
            symptoms, belt tension or tracking, a drive belt, rollers, or a belt and deck service.
            These are cheap parts on a sound machine, and a belt-and-deck service transforms how an
            older treadmill feels.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Usually repair</strong> a controller board or a motor on a machine that is
            otherwise in good condition, has a heavy frame and a long deck, and has been maintained.
            Good hardware is worth putting money into.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Think hard</strong> about a console or upper board, especially on a
            platform-tied machine, and about any repair on a treadmill that was inexpensive new.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Replace</strong> when the frame is damaged, when several things have failed at
            once, when the machine ran dry for years, or when the repair quote approaches what a
            known-good machine costs. Our
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifespan
            guide</a> covers how to read a cluster of faults as an ending rather than a coincidence.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The test that settles it:</strong> would you pay the repair cost for this exact
            machine, in working order, at its current age, with no warranty? If no, the repair is
            not worth doing however reasonable the part price looks on its own.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>And check the used market before deciding.</strong> Our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used analysis</a>
            shows what a few hundred dollars buys secondhand, and there are situations where the
            repair cost and a better machine are the same number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="preventing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Preventing the Next One</h2>
<p>
            Nearly every expensive repair in this guide traces back to one variable, and it is not
            age or brand.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Friction between belt and deck is the root cause.</strong> As the deck's
            low-friction surface wears and the belt dries, drag rises. The motor draws more current
            to hold the same speed. The controller board runs hotter. Both age faster, and eventually
            one fails — and the bill for that is an order of magnitude more than the lubricant would
            have been. Our
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">interval
            guide</a> covers the schedule and our
            <a href="/can-you-use-wd40-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to
            what not to use</a> covers the common ways people make it worse.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Four habits cover almost everything:</strong> lubricate on schedule, keep the
            belt centred and correctly tensioned, vacuum the motor compartment twice a year, and
            keep the machine off deep carpet where the housing cannot breathe — our
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">carpet guide</a>
            covers why that matters and a
            <a href="/treadmill-mat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">proper mat</a> is the usual
            answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance
            checklist</a> is the schedule version of that</strong>, and the whole routine is well
            under an hour a year.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Fix small things early.</strong> A belt slightly off centre wears its own edge
            and then needs replacing. A squeak ignored becomes a bearing. Nearly every large treadmill
            bill started as a ten-minute job somebody put off.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does treadmill repair cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Technicians commonly charge $75 to $150 for a call-out or diagnostic, often credited against the work, with labour at a similar hourly rate. Typical all-in repairs: a belt and deck service runs a few hundred dollars, a controller board similar, a motor replacement often $400 to $800. A proprietary touchscreen console can exceed what the machine is worth. Figures vary by region and brand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth repairing a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always for belts, decks, rollers, wiring, safety keys and anything lubrication-related — cheap parts on a sound machine. Usually for a motor or controller board on well-built, well-maintained hardware. Rarely when the frame is damaged, several things have failed at once, or the quote approaches the cost of a known-good replacement. The test: would you pay that amount for this machine, working, at its current age, with no warranty?
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why won’t my treadmill turn on?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most often the safety key, whose magnet weakens over time, or a broken conductor in the wiring loom at a folding deck’s pivot. Also check the outlet with another device, the machine’s own reset breaker near the power inlet, the household breaker, and remove any extension lead. These cost nothing to check and account for a large share of apparent console failures.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I repair a treadmill myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Lubrication, belt tensioning and centring, safety keys, vacuuming the motor compartment, cleaning under the belt and replacing a running belt or flipping a deck are all realistic with ordinary tools. Rollers, drive belts and like-for-like board swaps are reasonable if you photograph everything first. Call someone for diagnosis rather than replacement, for mains-side wiring, and whenever you would be guessing between two expensive parts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I find a good treadmill repair technician?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with the manufacturer, which usually maintains a network of authorised servicers and has the parts channel. Then try commercial fitness servicers — every area with gyms has them, and many take domestic work between calls. Ask about the diagnostic fee, whether it is credited, experience with your brand, and whether they carry parts on the van or order after diagnosis.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most common expensive treadmill failure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The motor controller board, usually followed by the motor itself — and both are typically the downstream result of the same thing rather than independent faults. As the deck wears and the belt dries, friction rises, the motor draws more current, and the board runs hotter. Years of that ages both. It is why lubrication, which costs tens of dollars a year, prevents repairs costing hundreds.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When a Treadmill Fails</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first two cost people money on machines that were never really broken, which is the most avoidable outcome here.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Condemning the board before checking the safety key and the loom</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A dead console is assumed to be a failed board far more often than it is one. Safety key magnets weaken, and the wiring at a folding deck’s pivot flexes thousands of times until a conductor breaks inside intact-looking insulation. Both present exactly like dead electronics, both cost very little, and both are ruled out in ten minutes before anyone is called.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating rising friction as a motor problem</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A machine that has become noisy, slips, hesitates under load or feels like it is struggling is very often a dry deck rather than a failing motor. Slide a hand between belt and deck — dry, hot or gritty means friction is the cause. Lubricate and retest before authorising anything, because the same symptoms genuinely do precede motor failure if left long enough.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for a call-out and only fixing one thing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The diagnostic fee and the travel are fixed costs, so the marginal cost of additional work during the same visit is small. If a technician is already there for a board, have them check and service the belt, deck, rollers and tension too. Two visits for jobs that could have been one is the most common way repair bills get inflated.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not having the model and serial number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It is on a plate on the frame, usually near the front of the deck or on a rear crossbar. Every useful conversation about parts starts there, specifications vary within a model name between years, and a technician given it on the phone can often predict the fault and the cost before arriving. Photograph it now and keep it with the receipt.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

