        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Before anything else, <strong class="text-white">check the safety key, the outlet and
            breaker, the wiring at the fold pivot, and whether the deck is dry</strong>. Those four
            checks cost nothing, take ten minutes, and resolve a large share of treadmills that
            appear to be dead.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If it is genuinely broken, repair cheap parts on good hardware without hesitation and apply
            the test to everything else: would you pay that for this machine, working, at this age,
            with no warranty? Then
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">lubricate on schedule</a>
            — nearly every expensive repair here is the bill for skipping it.
          </p>
        </div>
